(727430, 75565, 20992, '<td valign="top">:: Critique Club Comment ::<br/> <br/>A lot has already been said in other comments, so I''ll try to put things together shortly:<br/>You have put the word ''Amish'' in the center, so this is obviously meant to be the center of attention. You did it by photographing the sign from a different perspective, wich is appealing to the viewer, because it brings a good tension in the picture. What is a pity, is that the "A" of "Amish" is not wholy showing. <br/> <br/>The sunlight is overexposing the sign, making te colours weak. The picture could have been making a stronger expression, when you wouldn''t have been photographing into the sun. Maybe you could have waited until the sunlight was coming from another postition, when it would have shined ON the sign, the colours would have lit up and be bright!<br/> <br/>You made this picture for the challenge "something new", a lot of viewers commented that it isn''t clear what is new in your picture. I don''t see it either, maybe it''s something local or only know to you? Keep in mind that your public is very broad and living in all parts of the world, so a subject must speak to a lot of people.<br/> <br/>Fotowereld.</td>', ':: Critique Club Comment :: A lot has already been said in other comments, so I''ll try to put things together shortly:You have put the word ''Amish'' in the center, so this is obviously meant to be the center of attention. You did it by photographing the sign from a different perspective, wich is appealing to the viewer, because it brings a good tension in the picture. What is a pity, is that the "A" of "Amish" is not wholy showing.  The sunlight is overexposing the sign, making te colours weak. The picture could have been making a stronger expression, when you wouldn''t have been photographing into the sun. Maybe you could have waited until the sunlight was coming from another postition, when it would have shined ON the sign, the colours would have lit up and be bright! You made this picture for the challenge "something new", a lot of viewers commented that it isn''t clear what is new in your picture. I don''t see it either, maybe it''s something local or only know to you? Keep in mind that your public is very broad and living in all parts of the world, so a subject must speak to a lot of people. Fotowereld.', '2004-05-19 16:45:48.000000', NULL, 0),
(727566, 75234, 19780, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club. <br/> <br/>Composition: I think the subject of your photo is very compelling, but the framing of it is the weakest part of the photo. Keeping the trunk of the tree off-centered is a great choice, but perhaps an additional off centered line would help (the "eye" is pretty much centered). <br/> <br/>Background: The muted and dark greenery is a great background for this piece. <br/> <br/>Camera Work: (Focus/DOF/Exposure) The DOF with the mushed leaves is wonderful. The colors and exposure are good, but I think the textures of the eye would be served by a crisper focus. <br/> <br/>Post Processing: the in camera exposure is good, there was no need to do any other post except for maybe a more creative crop and better use of positive/negative space. <br/> <br/>The Challenge: n/a<br/> <br/>My Opinion: I like the colors and textures you found here. I like the subject much better than the execution, but luckily that can be easily remedied, you have a good eye for color and the use of DOF was great. Cropping the top more radically actually gives it more of an "eye" feel because it allows us to imagine an eyebrow. <br/> <br/>I hope these comments have been of help to you. Please mark them as helpful if they have, and feel free to PM me if you want to discuss or clarify anything here.<br/> <br/>(edited for spelling)<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2004-05-19 18:54:34.</i></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club.  Composition: I think the subject of your photo is very compelling, but the framing of it is the weakest part of the photo. Keeping the trunk of the tree off-centered is a great choice, but perhaps an additional off centered line would help (the "eye" is pretty much centered).  Background: The muted and dark greenery is a great background for this piece.  Camera Work: (Focus/DOF/Exposure) The DOF with the mushed leaves is wonderful. The colors and exposure are good, but I think the textures of the eye would be served by a crisper focus.  Post Processing: the in camera exposure is good, there was no need to do any other post except for maybe a more creative crop and better use of positive/negative space.  The Challenge: n/a My Opinion: I like the colors and textures you found here. I like the subject much better than the execution, but luckily that can be easily remedied, you have a good eye for color and the use of DOF was great. Cropping the top more radically actually gives it more of an "eye" feel because it allows us to imagine an eyebrow.  I hope these comments have been of help to you. Please mark them as helpful if they have, and feel free to PM me if you want to discuss or clarify anything here. (edited for spelling)', '2004-05-19 17:20:45.000000', '2004-05-19 19:54:34.000000', 0),
(727845, 74259, 1368, '<td valign="top">Omg I see the animal spirits in the log! Its so obvious when its pointed out! Awesome man!!!</td>', 'Omg I see the animal spirits in the log! Its so obvious when its pointed out! Awesome man!!!', '2004-05-19 19:06:57.000000', NULL, 0),
(728054, 75631, 7743, '<td valign="top">** Critique Club **<br/>  by cpanaioti<br/> <br/>Capturing this type of image is not always easy.  What you see with your eye is not necessarily what the camera sees.  You''ve done a good job of getting the contrast just right to bring out the light rays, however the brightest area still seems blown out.<br/> <br/>The dark clouds add interest, however the silhouetted bushes at the bottom don''t seem to add much.  Cropping out most of it, just above the power lines, would probably work and still keep the image balanced.<br/> <br/>Keep shooting.<br/> <br/>Colette</td>', '** Critique Club **  by cpanaioti Capturing this type of image is not always easy.  What you see with your eye is not necessarily what the camera sees.  You''ve done a good job of getting the contrast just right to bring out the light rays, however the brightest area still seems blown out. The dark clouds add interest, however the silhouetted bushes at the bottom don''t seem to add much.  Cropping out most of it, just above the power lines, would probably work and still keep the image balanced. Keep shooting. Colette', '2004-05-19 20:48:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(728168, 75231, 20395, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>First of all, excellent job with the photo. It''s a creative idea nicely executed. There are a few things though that I think would add to the picture.<br/> <br/>Compostion:<br/>This is the area I think you could improve the most. Like I said when I commented during the challenge, the strawberry on top of the kiwis is pretty muddy. I''m not sure you could have fixed this by thickening the strawberry slice as one commenter suggested. I think the strawberry would have turned out too dark, unless you used another light source, which might have gotten rid of the nice kiwi effect. I think the best bet would be to place the strawberry directly on the glass as well. Also, I might have moved the strawberry placement a little up and to the right to get the most out of the rule of thirds and add interest. <br/> <br/>Technical: This was a very strong aspect of the picture. The focus is sharp throughout and the lighting excellent (minus muddy strawberry). The exposure also appears to be dead on. <br/> <br/>Appeal: Typically I''m not a big fan of food pictures. They make me think of cheesy hotel art and mid-grade Italian restaurants. However, the creativity of this shot greatly enhances the appeal for me. That''s just my two cents though. <br/> <br/>Meeting the Challenge: It''s creative, it attempts something new, and it''s nothing like what you have already submitted. I think it works very well. <br/> <br/>In closing, I think that if you would be willing to go through the pain of thinly slicing kiwi again to take another crack at this shot with a non-muddy strawberry (or maybe a more translucent fruit? Oranges? oooh... that could be cool) I think it would be worth your while. <br/> <br/>Good job!<br/>Amanda</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! First of all, excellent job with the photo. It''s a creative idea nicely executed. There are a few things though that I think would add to the picture. Compostion:This is the area I think you could improve the most. Like I said when I commented during the challenge, the strawberry on top of the kiwis is pretty muddy. I''m not sure you could have fixed this by thickening the strawberry slice as one commenter suggested. I think the strawberry would have turned out too dark, unless you used another light source, which might have gotten rid of the nice kiwi effect. I think the best bet would be to place the strawberry directly on the glass as well. Also, I might have moved the strawberry placement a little up and to the right to get the most out of the rule of thirds and add interest.  Technical: This was a very strong aspect of the picture. The focus is sharp throughout and the lighting excellent (minus muddy strawberry). The exposure also appears to be dead on.  Appeal: Typically I''m not a big fan of food pictures. They make me think of cheesy hotel art and mid-grade Italian restaurants. However, the creativity of this shot greatly enhances the appeal for me. That''s just my two cents though.  Meeting the Challenge: It''s creative, it attempts something new, and it''s nothing like what you have already submitted. I think it works very well.  In closing, I think that if you would be willing to go through the pain of thinly slicing kiwi again to take another crack at this shot with a non-muddy strawberry (or maybe a more translucent fruit? Oranges? oooh... that could be cool) I think it would be worth your while.  Good job!Amanda', '2004-05-19 22:02:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(728190, 74765, 20395, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club, and your daughter! <br/> <br/>How weird out of all of the randomly alotted pictures, to be alloted yours for in-depth critique, hehehe...<br/> <br/>Composition: This seems to be the main area of concern for your commenters. I don''t think that the foreground is as distracting as they say, but I might have cropped in maybe a half inch on the bottom and an inch on the right hand side. Not in as far as one of your commenters suggested, but enough to draw the focus to the eggs a bit more. Try it- it might throw off the balance a little though. <br/> <br/>TECHNICAL: (COMPUTER HAS GONE HAYWIRE AND CAPSLOCK WONT RELEASE) A LITTLE MORE DOF MIGHT HAVE HELPED WITH THE FOREGROUND, BUT RIGHT NOW IT FOCUSES NICELY ON THE EGGS. THE EXPOSURE SEEMS RIGHT ON, KEEPING THE COLORS RICH. I MIGHT DO A LIGHT BURN ON THE BRIGHT PARTS OF THE NEST NOW THAT YOU CAN EDIT IT AS YOU PLEASE. <br/> <br/>APPEAL: IT APPEALS TO ME, BUT MANY OF THE VOTERS SEEM TO HAVE FOUND IT PRETTY MIDDLE OF THE ROAD. IT''S POSSIBLE THAT THEY EXPECTED SOMETHING MORE "WACKY" OR OUT THERE FOR THE SOMETHING NEW CHALLENGE. <br/> <br/>FITTING THE CHALLENGE: I''VE NEVER SEEN YOU TAKE A PICTURE OF EGGS BEFORE, NOR HAVE I SEEN YOU CLIMBING TREES CAMERA IN HAND. <br/> <br/>SORRY FOR ALL THE YELLING. DARM WORK COMPUTER!<br/>AMANDA</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club, and your daughter!  How weird out of all of the randomly alotted pictures, to be alloted yours for in-depth critique, hehehe... Composition: This seems to be the main area of concern for your commenters. I don''t think that the foreground is as distracting as they say, but I might have cropped in maybe a half inch on the bottom and an inch on the right hand side. Not in as far as one of your commenters suggested, but enough to draw the focus to the eggs a bit more. Try it- it might throw off the balance a little though.  TECHNICAL: (COMPUTER HAS GONE HAYWIRE AND CAPSLOCK WONT RELEASE) A LITTLE MORE DOF MIGHT HAVE HELPED WITH THE FOREGROUND, BUT RIGHT NOW IT FOCUSES NICELY ON THE EGGS. THE EXPOSURE SEEMS RIGHT ON, KEEPING THE COLORS RICH. I MIGHT DO A LIGHT BURN ON THE BRIGHT PARTS OF THE NEST NOW THAT YOU CAN EDIT IT AS YOU PLEASE.  APPEAL: IT APPEALS TO ME, BUT MANY OF THE VOTERS SEEM TO HAVE FOUND IT PRETTY MIDDLE OF THE ROAD. IT''S POSSIBLE THAT THEY EXPECTED SOMETHING MORE "WACKY" OR OUT THERE FOR THE SOMETHING NEW CHALLENGE.  FITTING THE CHALLENGE: I''VE NEVER SEEN YOU TAKE A PICTURE OF EGGS BEFORE, NOR HAVE I SEEN YOU CLIMBING TREES CAMERA IN HAND.  SORRY FOR ALL THE YELLING. DARM WORK COMPUTER!AMANDA', '2004-05-19 22:24:01.000000', NULL, 0),
